I see your point. The whole repo is [here](https://github.com/guardian/comment-count-widget)

`Something` is actually

``` js
export function CommentCount ({ fail }) {
    return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
        if (fail) {
            reject(new Error('Error loading comment count'));
        } else {
            const widgets = document.getElementsByTagName('comment-count');
            Array.from(widgets).forEach(fill);
            resolve();
        }
    });
}

function fill (element) {
    element.innerText = 42;
}
```

Iterating on DOM nodes to set the innerText is a side effect, I guess

I was just having the exact same issue. I made a gist to help out in cases where I really do need the function.
https://gist.github.com/halfnibble/13b279b9214ee2c4382f29cd4d6ca7dd

The code is simply:

``` javascript
export var loadModule = function(module) {
    // Trick Rollup.js to keep JavaScript.
    let obj = {
        method: function() {}
    };
    obj.method(module);
};
```

Then, in your case:

``` javascript
import { CommentCount } from './lib';
import { loadModule } from './loadModule';

loadModule(CommentCount);
```

For some reason, passing an otherwise eliminated object to an object's method keeps it from being **_treeshooked_** out. (Yes, I just made that term up).

